Q: What&#39;s a proper way to handle a constructor process that might fail? try catch at the class instantiation? private constructor + public static member? A: <p>Look at what your constructor actually does:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>Class1::Class1(int option) {
  std::ifstream f;
  f.open(F_NAME_1.c_str());
  if (!f.is_open()) [[unlikely]] { throw std::runtime_error(/*...*/); }
  std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;file &quot; &lt;&lt; F_NAME_1 &lt;&lt; &quot; opened without errors\n&quot;;
  f.close();
}
</code></pre>
<p>It assigns nothing to a member. The object that it builds holds no state. The constructor is a procedure with a side effect, and the class is a place to put it.</p>
<p>This is the root of your question. A constructor exists to establish an invariant. Yours has no invariant to establish. Each later decision therefore rests on a class that does not yet earn its place. That covers the private access, the factory, and the position of the <code>try</code>.</p>
<h2>Separate the read from the construction</h2>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>struct Config { /* the values from the file */ };

Config load_config(const std::string&amp; path);   // this can fail

class Class1 {
public:
    explicit Class1(Config c) : cfg_(std::move(c)) {}   // this cannot fail
private:
    Config cfg_;
};
</code></pre>
<p>The failure now belongs to a free function, which is free to return a value. The constructor takes data that is already valid, so it has no failure mode, and your original question disappears.</p>
<p>This is the advice in the comment from Eljay, and it deserves an answer rather than a comment.</p>
<h2>Which form should <code>load_config</code> use</h2>
<p>The choice between an exception and <code>std::expected</code> has a rule, and your own code supplies the answer.</p>
<p>Throw when the immediate caller cannot do anything useful. Return a value when the caller has a real recovery path.</p>
<p>Your <code>choose_option</code> offers cancel, retry, and exit. The caller has a recovery path, and a file that is absent is a normal condition in your program, not a surprise. So return the failure:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>std::expected&lt;Config, ConfigError&gt; load_config(const std::string&amp; path);
</code></pre>
<p>An exception here makes the caller reconstruct, from a <code>catch</code> block, a decision that the direct return already gave it.</p>
<h2>What the private constructor costs you</h2>
<p>The other answer says that your factory adds nothing. It also takes something away.</p>
<p>A private constructor blocks each facility that constructs in place, because those facilities are not friends of your class:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>std::vector&lt;Class1&gt; v;
v.emplace_back(1);          // will not compile
std::make_unique&lt;Class1&gt;(1); // will not compile
std::optional&lt;Class1&gt; o;
o.emplace(1);               // will not compile
</code></pre>
<p>Make the constructor private only when you must reject direct construction. Do not make it private as a side effect of a factory that you added for another reason.</p>
<h2>A separate bug in the posted code</h2>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>int input1;
std::cin &gt;&gt; input1;
</code></pre>
<p>Type a letter. The extraction fails, <code>input1</code> becomes 0, and the bad character stays in the buffer. Your <code>else if (input1 &lt; 1 || input1 &gt; 2)</code> branch prints the message and continues. The next extraction fails at once, on the same character, and the loop spins forever.</p>
<p>The same fault is in the <code>catch</code> block, with <code>input2</code>. Test the stream and clear it:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>if (!(std::cin &gt;&gt; input1)) {
    std::cin.clear();
    std::cin.ignore(std::numeric_limits&lt;std::streamsize&gt;::max(), '\n');
    continue;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Also catch <code>const std::exception&amp;</code>, not <code>const std::runtime_error&amp;</code>. You depend today on a derivation chain that a later change to <code>std::filesystem</code> or to stream exceptions can move out from under you.</p>

Q: Add Timestamp to multiple cells when updated A: <p>You are using <a href="https://developers.google.com/apps-script/reference/spreadsheet/sheet#getrangerow,-column" rel="nofollow noreferrer">getRange(row, column)</a> which retrieves a single cell.</p>
<p>The following lines are setting only first cell's value in the range when you copy paste multiple values or pull down a cell:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>var cell = sheet.getRange(index, dateCol + 1);</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>cell.setValue(date);</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Use <a href="https://developers.google.com/apps-script/reference/spreadsheet/sheet#getRange(Integer,Integer,Integer)" rel="nofollow noreferrer">getRange(row, column, numRows)</a> to retrieve a range with multiple rows. If we change it with:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>sheet.getRange (index, dateCol + 1, numRows)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>it will change all cells in this range.</p>
<p>Try this please.</p>
<pre><code>function onEdit(event) { 
  var timezone = &quot;GMT+1&quot;;
  var timestamp_format = &quot;dd/MM/yyyy HH:mm:ss&quot;; // Timestamp Format. 
  var updateColName = &quot;Message&quot;;
  var timeStampColName = &quot;Timestamp&quot;;
  var sheet = event.source.getSheetByName('Sheet1'); //Name of the sheet where you want to run this script.    
  var actRng = event.range;
  var editColumn = actRng.getColumn();
  var index = actRng.getRowIndex();
  var numRows = actRng.getNumRows();
  var headers = sheet.getRange(1, 1, 1, sheet.getLastColumn()).getValues();
  var dateCol = headers[0].indexOf(timeStampColName);
  var updateCol = headers[0].indexOf(updateColName)+1; 
  if (dateCol &gt; -1 &amp;&amp; index &gt; 1 &amp;&amp; editColumn == updateCol) { 
    var range = sheet.getRange(index, dateCol + 1,numRows);
    var date = Utilities.formatDate(new Date(), timezone, timestamp_format);
    range.setValue(date);
  }
}
</code></pre>
<h3 id="reference-cb52">Reference:</h3>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://developers.google.com/apps-script/reference/spreadsheet/sheet#getrangerow,-column" rel="nofollow noreferrer">getRange(row, column) </a></li>
<li><a href="https://developers.google.com/apps-script/reference/spreadsheet/sheet#getRange(Integer,Integer,Integer)" rel="nofollow noreferrer">getRange(row, column, numRows)</a></li>
</ul>

Q: jQuery sortable table rows are overflowing their container while moving A: <p><code>width:inherit</code> won't do the work. If you inspect the dev tool you will notice the dragging item will be set to <code>position:absolute</code> to have its position be controlled by JQuery's dragging logic.</p>
<p>You just need to update the container that you want as a boundary to be positioned. (Choosing <code>.responsive-table</code> makes most sense because that's the scroll container.)</p>
<p>Then, since you now hide the overflow, you probably would want to use the <a href="https://api.jqueryui.com/sortable/#option-containment" rel="nofollow noreferrer">containment</a> option to make sure it doesn't still allow user to drag it anywhere in the screen. Use the same boundary container instead of <code>parent</code> works better to me, but you're free to use the value <code>parent</code> (which is the <code>tbody</code> in your example) or another container.</p>
<pre><code>/*Hide the overflow part under `table-responsive`*/
.table-responsive {
  position: relative;
}

/*Set containment*/
$(&quot;.sortable&quot;).each(function() {
  const targetContainment = $(this).closest(&quot;table&quot;);
  $(this).sortable({
    axis: &quot;y&quot;,
    classes: {
      &quot;ui-sortable-helper&quot;: &quot;inherit-width&quot;
    },
    helper: &quot;clone&quot;,
    containment: targetContainment,
  })
})
</code></pre>
<p>See example below:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>$(".sortable").each(function() {
  const targetContainment = $(this).closest("table");
  $(this).sortable({
    axis: "y",
    classes: {
      "ui-sortable-helper": "d-table"
    },
    helper: "clone",
    containment: targetContainment,

  })
})</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>table * {
  white-space: nowrap;
}

.table-responsive {
  position: relative;
}

.d-table {
  display: table
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.3.3/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css"&gt;
&lt;script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.7.1.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.3.3/dist/js/bootstrap.bundle.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script src="https://code.jquery.com/ui/1.14.1/jquery-ui.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;div class="container"&gt;
  &lt;div class="table-responsive"&gt;
    &lt;table class="table table-bordered"&gt;
      &lt;thead&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;
          &lt;th&gt;one&lt;/th&gt;
          &lt;th&gt;two&lt;/th&gt;
          &lt;th&gt;three&lt;/th&gt;
          &lt;th&gt;four&lt;/th&gt;
          &lt;th&gt;five&lt;/th&gt;
        &lt;/tr&gt;
      &lt;/thead&gt;
      &lt;tbody class="sortable"&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;foo foo foo foo foo&lt;/td&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;foo foo foo foo foo&lt;/td&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;foo foo foo foo foo &lt;/td&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;foo foo foo foo foo&lt;/td&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;foo foo foo foo foo&lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;bar bar bar bar bar&lt;/td&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;bar bar bar bar bar&lt;/td&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;bar bar bar bar bar&lt;/td&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;bar bar bar bar bar&lt;/td&gt;
          &lt;td&gt;bar bar bar bar bar&lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;/tr&gt;
      &lt;/tbody&gt;
    &lt;/table&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Note that by using <code>clone</code>, you are also free to set the dragging item to have <code>max-width:xxx!important;overflow:hidden;</code> if you want to make it perfectly align with the container if the user hold and drag to the right. You can also create hidden clone to make sure the column layout don't shrink or grow during dragging using the drag <a href="https://api.jqueryui.com/sortable/#event-start" rel="nofollow noreferrer">start</a> and <a href="https://api.jqueryui.com/sortable/#event-stop" rel="nofollow noreferrer">stop</a> events.</p>

Q: How to resize a JFrame to include the full title text? A: <p>As mentioned, the title bar of the <code>JFrame</code> is provided by the host operating system and is also influenced by the <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/uiswing/lookandfeel/plaf.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">look-and-feel</a> you are using. One alternative is to create an <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.desktop/java/awt/Frame.html#setUndecorated(boolean)" rel="nofollow noreferrer">undecorated</a> <code>JFrame</code> and handle the decorations yourself – or use <a href="https://www.formdev.com/flatlaf/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">FlatLaf</a> which provides ready-made code for making undecorated windows and providing decorations for them.<br />
Below code shows how to achieve making the <code>JFrame</code> wide enough so as to display the entire <code>JFrame</code> title without ellipsis – with the help of FlatLaf.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>import com.formdev.flatlaf.FlatDarkLaf;
import com.formdev.flatlaf.ui.FlatTitlePane;

import java.awt.BorderLayout;
import java.awt.Component;
import java.awt.Dimension;
import java.awt.EventQueue;
import java.awt.Insets;
import java.awt.Window;
import java.awt.event.WindowEvent;
import java.awt.event.WindowListener;

import javax.swing.JFrame;
import javax.swing.JLabel;
import javax.swing.JLayeredPane;
import javax.swing.JRootPane;

public class FlatLaf0 implements Runnable, WindowListener {
/* Start 'Runnable' interface methods. */
    @Override // java.lang.Runnable
    public void run() {
        createAndDisplayGui();
    }
/* End 'Runnable' interface methods. */
/* Start 'WindowListener' interface methods. */
    @Override // java.awt.event.WindowListener
    public void windowActivated(WindowEvent e) {
        // Do nothing.
    }

    @Override // java.awt.event.WindowListener
    public void windowClosed(WindowEvent e) {
        // Do nothing.
    }

    @Override // java.awt.event.WindowListener
    public void windowClosing(WindowEvent e) {
        // Do nothing.
    }

    @Override // java.awt.event.WindowListener
    public void windowDeactivated(WindowEvent e) {
        // Do nothing.
    }

    @Override // java.awt.event.WindowListener
    public void windowDeiconified(WindowEvent e) {
        // Do nothing.
    }

    @Override // java.awt.event.WindowListener
    public void windowIconified(WindowEvent e) {
        // Do nothing.
    }

    @Override // java.awt.event.WindowListener
    public void windowOpened(WindowEvent e) {
        Window window = e.getWindow();
        if (window instanceof JFrame frame) {
            int titleWidth = 0;
            JRootPane rootPane = frame.getRootPane();
            Component[] cmpts = rootPane.getComponents();
            found:
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; cmpts.length; i++) {
                if (cmpts[i] instanceof JLayeredPane layeredPane) {
                    Component[] lpCmpts = layeredPane.getComponents();
                    for (int j = 0; j &lt; lpCmpts.length; j++) {
                        if (lpCmpts[j] instanceof FlatTitlePane titlePane) {
                            Dimension titleDim = titlePane.getPreferredSize();
                            titleWidth = titleDim.width;
                            break found;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            Insets insets = frame.getInsets();
            int insetsWidth = insets.left + insets.right;
            frame.setSize(titleWidth + insetsWidth, frame.getHeight());
        }
    }
/* End 'WindowListener' interface methods. */
    private void createAndDisplayGui() {
        JFrame frame = new JFrame(&quot;This is a very looooooooooooooooooooooong title&quot;);
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
        frame.addWindowListener(this);
        JLabel label = new JLabel(&quot;JLabel&quot;);
        frame.add(label, BorderLayout.CENTER);
        frame.pack();
        frame.setLocationByPlatform(true);
        frame.setVisible(true);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FlatDarkLaf.setup();
        EventQueue.invokeLater(new FlatLaf0());
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Method <code>windowOpened()</code> is called once only – when the <code>JFrame</code> is initially displayed.</p>
<p>This is how it looks on my Windows 10 with Java 26.</p>
